.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt OES 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm oes
.Nd OpenEndpointSecurity event monitoring and authorization framework
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the driver as a module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
oes_load="YES"
.Ed
.Pp
The module can only be loaded during boot and cannot be unloaded.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
framework provides endpoint-security event monitoring and authorization,
inspired by Apple's Endpoint Security API but built on the
.Fx
MAC framework and the Capsicum capability model.
It registers a MAC policy whose hooks turn kernel security-relevant operations
into events, and exposes them to userland clients through the
.Pa /dev/oes
character device.
The
.Xr liboes 3
library is the supported client interface.
The kernel interface is LP64-only.
It is unavailable to 32-bit kernels and processes, and no 32-bit ioctl
translation ABI is provided.
.Pp
Events are of two kinds.
.Bl -tag -width "NOTIFY" -offset indent
.It AUTH
Authorization events are delivered from sleepable MAC check hooks; a subscribed
client may allow or deny the operation, and the operation blocks until the
client responds or the message deadline expires.
.It NOTIFY
Notification events are informational and never block the originating thread.
When a NOTIFY event is the counterpart of an AUTH event, it is delivered after
the combined authorization decision and includes that applied decision.
Such an AUTH-derived notification is emitted before the MAC check returns; it
reports the authorization outcome, not proof that the system call subsequently
succeeded.
This differs from a post-operation notification.
.El
.Pp
Multiple clients, in different processes, may be active at once.
Each
.Xr open 2
of
.Pa /dev/oes
creates an independent client with its own subscription set, muting state, mode,
and decision cache; events fan out to every subscribed client.
The maximum number of concurrent clients is bounded by the
.Va security.oes.max_clients
tunable.
.Pp
A client subscribes to event types with the
.Dv OES_IOC_SUBSCRIBE
ioctl, reads events with
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr select 2 ,
or
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
and answers authorization events by writing an
.Vt oes_response_t
or
.Vt oes_response_flags_t .
Processes and paths may be muted per client so that their events are suppressed.
Path muting matches the primary event object; target-path muting matches a
secondary or destination object.
It does not mean "events originating from
this executable" as similarly named APIs on some other systems do.
.Pp
A client may set
.Dv OES_SCOPE_DESCENDANTS
before setting its mode or subscriptions.
Such a client receives NOTIFY events for its opener and AUTH plus NOTIFY events
for the opener's entire descendant subtree, including descendants that already
exist when the scope is selected.
All other processes are invisible, and process mute operations are rejected
for processes outside the visible subtree.
The default self mute is not applied to this scope because it would suppress
the required root-process notifications.
.Pp
A client may instead select a jail scope with
.Dv OES_IOC_SET_JAIL_SCOPE ,
also before setting its mode or subscriptions.
The argument,
.Vt struct oes_jail_scope_args ,
names a jail by jail ID
.Pq Va ejs_jid
or, with
.Dv OES_JAIL_SCOPE_BYNAME ,
by name
.Pq Va ejs_name .
Jail ID 0 is the caller's own jail, which is the host system for an unjailed
caller.
The jail is looked up relative to the caller's jail: a jailed caller can name
only its own jail, as jail ID 0, or a jail beneath it.
Such a client receives AUTH and NOTIFY events only for threads whose
credential is, when the event is generated, in that jail or in a jail nested
beneath it;
.Dv OES_JAIL_SCOPE_NOCHILDREN
excludes nested jails.
An AUTH client that uses it does not supervise a process once that process
moves into a nested jail; use it for AUTH only on jails that cannot have
children.
Membership is evaluated for every event, so processes that enter the jail
later with
.Xr jail_attach 2
are covered from their first system call after it returns, and ancestry is
irrelevant.
Only the thread performing the operation counts: an operation by an outside
process on a file, socket or process inside the jail is not delivered, and an
AUTH client with a jail scope never delays a process outside its jail.
A kernel service acting for a process is judged by the credential it acts
under.
With
.Dv OES_JAIL_SCOPE_INBOUND
the NOTIFY signal, ptrace and scheduling events whose target process is in the
jail are delivered as well, although their source is outside it; a jailed
caller may not request this.
Process mute operations are rejected for processes outside the scope, and the
mutes that are set select among the events in scope.
.Pp
The kernel keeps a reference to the jail itself, so a jail later created with
the same ID or name, or a renamed jail, never changes what the scope matches.
When the jail is removed the scope is dead and cannot be revived.
Every event that the jail's processes generated before the removal began,
the exit of its last process included, is still delivered, subject to the
ordinary queue limits; then
.Xr read 2
returns 0,
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLHUP
and an
.Dv EVFILT_READ
kevent carries
.Dv EV_EOF .
AUTH events already delivered can still be answered.
When
.Xr jail_remove 2
kills processes that are still running, their supervision ends while they
die: the operations they have in flight and their exits may or may not be
delivered, and are no longer held for an AUTH decision once the scope is dead.
.Dv OES_IOC_GET_JAIL_SCOPE
returns the flags, with
.Dv OES_JAIL_SCOPE_DEAD
once the scope is dead, and the jail ID and name as the scope was set:
a caller that named its own jail reads back jail ID 0 and the name
.Dq 0 .
They are left zero for a jailed caller that is not in the jail the scope was
set from, a scope set from outside any jail included.
Event records are not adjusted for the reader: a jailed reader sees the jail
IDs and hierarchical jail names of the host's view.
It fails with
.Er ENOENT
for a client without a jail scope.
A scope cannot be changed once set, and the descendants and jail scopes cannot
be combined.
.Pp
.Dv OES_IOC_SET_JAIL_SCOPE
fails with
.Er EINVAL
for malformed arguments,
.Er EBUSY
if the client is already configured,
.Er EPERM
if the client already has a scope or a jailed caller requests
.Dv OES_JAIL_SCOPE_INBOUND ,
and
.Er ENOENT
if no such live jail is visible to the caller, in that order.
.Pp
While a jail-scoped client is open every event records the jail of its
subject, at the cost of one reference on that jail for the life of the
event; the host itself is not reference counted.
While every open client is jail-scoped, events of subjects that none of them
covers are discarded before any memory is allocated, any path is resolved or
any lock that can sleep is taken.
Two kinds of event are exempt and take the ordinary path, without being
delivered to or waited on by the jail-scoped clients: AUTH events while
.Va security.oes.require_auth_clients ,
.Va security.oes.auth_fail_closed
or a fail-closed
.Va security.oes.default_deadline_miss_mode
is set, and events that invalidate cached decisions while a jail-scoped client
has cached any.
Under the first two settings an AUTH operation outside the jail is then denied
unless some other client authorizes it: a jail-scoped client does not satisfy
a strict policy for the rest of the system.
A client with any other scope, descendants included, switches this off.
.Pp
Per-event AUTH deadlines may be configured with
.Dv OES_IOC_SET_DEADLINE_MAX
and queried with
.Dv OES_IOC_GET_DEADLINE_MAX .
Descendants-scoped clients may additionally set and query deadline floors with
.Dv OES_IOC_SET_DEADLINE_MIN
and
.Dv OES_IOC_GET_DEADLINE_MIN .
A zero SET value removes the event override.
If a minimum exceeds the current maximum, the effective maximum rises to the
minimum; lowering a maximum below its minimum lowers the minimum as well.
The client-wide deadline in
.Vt oes_mode_args
is the inherited default.
.Dv OES_IOC_SET_DEADLINE_MISS_MODE
selects fail-open or fail-closed behavior for deadline misses and AUTH messages
that cannot be queued.
.Ss Event metadata
Each message contains monotonic and wall-clock timestamps, the triggering
thread ID and name when available, and a snapshot of the source process.
Process metadata includes current and
.Fx
real-parent IDs, process reaper,
process group and session, credentials and supplementary groups, audit session
and terminal data, jail and login-class identity, ABI, start time, process
state, thread count, nice value, OS release, and routing FIB.
The
.Dv EP_FLAG_OES_CLIENT
bit reports whether the source process owns an active OES client, analogous to
Apple's
.Va es_process_t.is_es_client
field.
.Pp
The executable path is captured during a successful exec and carried in the
credential label, so subsequent MAC hooks do not perform unsafe vnode path
lookups.
The path is inherited across fork and credential changes.
A process that predates OES activation may have
.Dv OES_PROC_META_PATH_UNAVAILABLE
set until its next exec.
Current-working-directory paths are also explicitly marked unavailable when
they cannot be captured without violating MAC-hook lock constraints.
.Pp
File objects include vnode identity, ownership, mode and flags, size, exact
allocated bytes, preferred block size, generation, special-device ID,
filesystem revision and type, link count, and second-plus-nanosecond access,
modification, change, and birth timestamps.
The attributes are read from the object when the event is generated.
The access timestamp of the executable in an exec event may already
include the kernel's own reads of the image during that exec, such as those
of
.Dv MAC_VERIEXEC
image verification, and may be later than the one the file had before the
exec began; the other timestamps are not changed by an exec.
The
.Dv OES_FILE_META_*
bits distinguish unavailable attributes, unavailable or truncated paths,
proposed attributes for objects that do not exist yet, and identities presented
by a
.Xr dq 4
controller rather than read from a vnode
.Pq Dv OES_FILE_META_PRESENTED .
.Ss MAC hooks
Events are generated from MAC policy hooks, including process execution and
credential changes, the file lifecycle
.Pq open, close, create, unlink, rename, link ,
memory mapping, extended attributes and ACLs, mounts, module load and unload,
and socket and pipe operations.
File
.Em close
events are delivered via the
.Fn mac_vnode_check_close
hook; because a close cannot be denied, close is a NOTIFY-only event.
A signal that a process sends to itself generates no event: the kernel
permits it before any MAC check is made.
A reboot event reports a request made with
.Xr reboot 2 ,
before the kernel has decided whether the caller may make it; it does not
say that the system is going down.
.Pp
Descriptors served by a
.Xr dq 4
controller have no vnode, so the vnode hooks never see them.
For those descriptors only, the policy implements the descriptor-level hooks
.Fn mac_file_check_read ,
.Fn mac_file_check_stat ,
.Fn mac_file_check_write ,
.Fn mac_file_check_setattr ,
.Fn mac_file_check_fsync ,
.Fn mac_file_check_lock ,
.Fn mac_file_check_ioctl ,
.Fn mac_file_check_mmap ,
.Fn mac_file_notify_close
and
.Fn mac_file_notify_reply ,
generating the AUTH events
.Dv OES_EVENT_AUTH_FILE_READ ,
.Dv _FILE_STAT ,
.Dv _FILE_WRITE ,
.Dv _FILE_SETATTR ,
.Dv _FILE_FSYNC ,
.Dv _FILE_LOCK ,
.Dv _FILE_IOCTL
and
.Dv _FILE_MMAP ,
each with its NOTIFY counterpart, and the NOTIFY-only
.Dv OES_EVENT_NOTIFY_FILE_CLOSE
and
.Dv OES_EVENT_NOTIFY_DQ_REPLY .
The file object in these messages is the identity the controller presents and
carries
.Dv OES_FILE_META_PRESENTED .
.Dv FILE_SETATTR
reports the requested changes in
.Vt oes_event_file_setattr_t ,
whose
.Va mask
of
.Dv OES_FILE_SETATTR_*
bits names the mode, owner, group, flags, size and access or modification
times present;
.Dv FILE_LOCK
reports the request and its
.Vt struct flock
fields in
.Vt oes_event_file_lock_t ;
.Dv FILE_IOCTL
reports the command in
.Vt oes_event_file_ioctl_t ;
.Dv FILE_MMAP
uses the
.Dv MMAP
payload with the requested address and length filled.
A
.Dv NOTIFY_DQ_REPLY
message is attributed to the controller process and reports the answered
operation, its errno, the object id, the controller's identity nonce and the
reply length; it never carries reply data.
Any other descriptor type returns from these hooks at once and produces no
event.
.Ss Idle behaviour
.Nm
costs nothing while nothing listens.
A hook is live while some open client subscribes to the AUTH or NOTIFY
event it produces; while
.Va security.oes.require_auth_clients ,
.Va security.oes.auth_fail_closed
or a fail-closed
.Va security.oes.default_deadline_miss_mode
is set, for every hook that produces an AUTH event; and, for a hook whose
event drops cached decisions, once some client has added a decision-cache
entry.
A client that has
.Pa /dev/oes
open and subscribes to nothing makes no hook live.
A hook that is not live returns at its first test, which is what it would
otherwise have returned, and counts toward none of the MAC framework's
fast-path flags, so with nothing listening
.Nm
makes the framework enter no system call path on its own account; on paths
another policy keeps hot its hooks are still called and return at once.
The credential, exec and pipe identities are kept whether or not anything
listens, so that a client starting later sees them.
.Pp
Liveness is recomputed before a subscribe or cache ioctl returns and before
a write of a strict setting returns: an operation that starts after the
call returns is delivered, consulted or denied as the new state requires.
Turning a strict setting on makes the AUTH hooks live before the new value
takes effect.
An operation already past its hook's first test when a client subscribes is
not reported, like one that started just before the subscription.
Closing the last client of an event makes its hooks idle without waiting
for threads inside them.
.Pp
.Nm
is compiled into the kernel and cannot be unloaded.
.Ss DTrace
The framework defines an
.Nm
SDT provider with probes for authorization outcomes
.Pq allow, deny, timeout ,
event enqueue and drop, and decision-cache hit and miss.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va security.oes.max_clients
Maximum number of concurrent clients.
.It Va security.oes.require_auth_clients
When non-zero, operations that would generate an AUTH event are denied if no
client is subscribed to authorize them.
.It Va security.oes.default_deadline_ms
Default client-wide AUTH deadline in milliseconds.
.It Va security.oes.default_deadline_miss_mode
Default deadline-miss behavior: zero is fail open and one is fail closed.
.It Va security.oes.default_self_mute
When non-zero, a new client is automatically muted against its own process.
This default is mutable client state and may be removed.
It is not applied to descendants-scoped clients; it is applied to jail-scoped
clients.
.It Va security.oes.jail_filtered
Number of events discarded before any memory was allocated for them because
every open client is jail-scoped and none covers the subject.
.It Va security.oes.jail_filtered_auth
The AUTH events among them.
.It Va security.oes.gate.hooks
One line per gated hook: index, name, the AUTH, NOTIFY and alternate event
numbers that make it live, whether its event drops cached decisions, its
MAC fast-path hook, and whether it is live
.Pq see Sx Idle behaviour .
.It Va security.oes.gate.live
The live hooks as a hexadecimal bit string, bit
.Ar n
for hook index
.Ar n .
.It Va security.oes.gate.passes
Number of hook entries that passed their first test.
.It Va security.oes.gate.reconciles
Number of liveness recomputations.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /dev/oes" -compact
.It Pa /dev/oes
client device
.El
